#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143670} :activity/id #uuid "57757a46-bbb2-5088-ae43-186d67d75fb2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050634 :topic/id #uuid "5a8cf7ea-858c-5b2b-83e2-c517e9b87bd9" :topic/math-academy-id 2379} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186144068 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to multiply a mixed number by a whole number:\n\n$$\n3\\times 2\\,\\dfrac 3{4}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we perform the following steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Convert both numbers to improper fractions. Here, the number $3$ can be expressed as the improper fraction\n\n$$\n3=\\dfrac{3}{1},\n$$\n\nand the mixed number $2\\,\\dfrac 3{4}$ can be expressed as the improper fraction\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 3{4}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}= \\dfrac {11} {4}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Multiply the resulting fractions together. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{1}\\times\\dfrac {11} {4}=  \\dfrac {3\\times 11} {1\\times 4} = \\dfrac{33}{4}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Simplify or convert back to a mixed number (if necessary):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{33}{4} =8\\,\\textrm{R} 1 = 8\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final result is\n\n$$\n3\\times 2\\,\\dfrac 3{4} = 8\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a377bce3-243a-5151-a804-db3a4901a8f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8559 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ff49a4a6-b4f3-58d2-abaf-f361c173cdda" :step/math-academy-id 135662 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142798 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142589 :question/id #uuid "54148f16-56b3-5ead-80fd-279c9b10ad29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13699"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d93a0632-2268-50e0-8fd9-2f42f7822759" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050249}] :knowl